<?nvs
// ADR 0116 § 2's teardown sweep, end to end: a program that builds object
// cycles in a loop and exits clean.
//
// Every pair below is unreachable the moment the loop goes round — nothing
// names it and nothing can reach it — and refcounting alone frees none of it,
// because each node holds the other above zero. What reclaims it is the sweep
// over the context's live list at teardown, which is why the WSL leg runs this
// fixture under `valgrind --leak-check=full`: the output would be identical
// either way, and only the checker can tell a program that let go of its
// memory from one that merely stopped mentioning it.
//
// The pair is the smallest cycle, and the third node makes it a cycle through a
// chain rather than a mutual pair, which is the shape a sweep that only handles
// self-reference would still get wrong.
//
// The isolate at the foot is the other half: a second context, torn down while
// this one keeps running, with an object crossing between the two.

class Node
{
    public string $label = '';
    public ?Node $peer;

    public function constructor(string $label)
    {
        $this->label = $label;
        $this->peer = null;
    }
}

var $built = 0;

for (int $i = 0; $i < 1000; $i += 1) {
    var $first = new Node('first');
    var $second = new Node('second');
    var $third = new Node('third');

    $first->peer = $second;
    $second->peer = $third;
    $third->peer = $first;

    $built += 1;
}

// The array-closed shape, which is the one a tally reading field slots alone
// could not see and which therefore stayed out for the life of the process.
// `$member`'s only reference is an *element* of `$holder`'s array, so the ring
// closes through the array rather than through a field; `$grid` is the same
// ring one array deeper, which is what pins the descent into a nested array
// the outer one solely owns. Neither loop touches `$built`, because this
// fixture's stdout is frozen by the acceptance check and what these iterations
// add is invisible in it — the WSL `valgrind --leak-check=full` leg is the
// whole check on them, exactly as it is on the pairs above.
class Ring
{
    public ?Ring $peer = null;
    public array<Ring> $group = [];
    public array<array<Ring>> $grid = [];
}

for (int $round = 0; $round < 1000; $round += 1) {
    var $holder = new Ring();
    var $member = new Ring();
    $holder->group[] = $member;
    $member->peer = $holder;

    var $outer = new Ring();
    var $inner = new Ring();
    $outer->grid[][] = $inner;
    $inner->peer = $outer;
}

// A node that points at itself, held to the end of the program: the cycle the
// sweep meets while the rest of the request is still being taken apart around
// it, rather than one abandoned a thousand iterations ago.
var $loop = new Node('loop');
$loop->peer = $loop;

echo 'built=', $built, "\n";

// The crossing, which is the half a single context cannot exercise: a fresh
// `Node` is the argument, so it crosses into the isolate at refcount one — a
// pointer handoff rather than a copy — and the child returns it after
// abandoning a cycle of its own. Reading it below is a read *after* that
// child's context was torn apart around it, which is what the WSL valgrind leg
// is here to watch: an object left on the wrong live list is one the child's
// sweep is free to dismantle under this program.
//
// The move this exercises is the one going in. The way back is a copy, because
// the child's own context holds the argument until it ends, so the value being
// returned is never the only reference to it; the source-to-destination move
// itself is pinned in Rust, by `nvs-runtime`'s
// `an_adopted_object_moves_to_the_destinations_live_list`.
var $child = spawn script 'cycles/child.nvs' with(
    args: new Node('crossed'),
    output: 'capture',
);
var $crossed = await $child;
if ($crossed->ok) {
    var $back = $crossed->value as Node;
    if ($back->label == 'crossed') {
        echo 'crossed=ok', "\n";
    } else {
        echo 'crossed=', $back->label, "\n";
    }
} else {
    echo 'crossed=', $crossed->error?->message, "\n";
}

echo 'done', "\n";
